			TinyMUD HELP Sheet			01/25/90


  TinyMUD is a user-extendible, multi-user adventure game.  The game's user
interface is like "adventure", or "zork", in that it consists entirely of
textual descriptions of rooms, objects, players, and exits.  It is completely
different from "adventure" and "zork" in that the game itself has no rooms,
objects, players, or exits.  These are all created by the players themselves.

  You may wander through parts of the game built by another, or create your own
adventure for others to play.  Players who chose to build are referred to here
as "builders", and the special commands they need above and beyond the General
Commands available to all are termed "Builder Commands".  Some versions check
a flag to see if a player is authorized to use these commands, most do not.

  In addition to the general commands which work anywhere in the game, the
builder may have created particular verbs or phrases in the room you are in
which can cause effects if the command is given, and if you are carrying or
not carrying the right objects.  An example would be if you are in a particular
room, holding a key, and the description says the door is locked.  The verb
"unlock door with key" may cause the door to open and you to enter the next
room.  The special meaning of the "unlock door with key" verb will likely be
limited to the room where it is useful.  General Commands and object names may
be abbreviated to the fewest characters which makes it distinguishable.

  You can play TinyMUD by connecting to a MUD server with "telnet".  Some
currently running games are listed below.  Some people find that using emacs
to connect allows them more flexibility, if you know emacs.  To disconnect
from the game at any time, one may type "QUIT".  This does not change where
your player is in the game -- the next time you connect, you will be exactly
where you were when you QUIT, with exactly what you were carrying, etc.
Because of this, if you will be gone from the game for extended periods, it is
considered good manners to drop any objects which you are carrying that others
might find useful while you are gone.  If you abuse this feature and don't drop
a needed object for a long time, the Wizard has the ability to take objects
away from you at any time and/or turn you into a toad.

  Since all exits from rooms are defined by the builder, the ways to get from
one place to another could be infinite.  Read the descriptions carefully,
they will usually give you clues as to where you can go from there.  Most
builders have been known to use compass directions, so you might try north,
south, east, west, ne, nw, se, sw, up, & down if you can't think of any others.
Some adventures are geographically consistent, others are not.


Available MUD's are reached by:
telnet 192.35.96.111 6250	(from an open subnet, this is TinyHell)
telnet 128.2.218.26 4201	(from an open subnet, this is Original TinyMud)

General Commands
----------------

DROP
drop <object>. Drops the specified object. Throw is the same as drop.

GET
get <object>. Gets the specified object. Take is the same as get.

GIVE
give <player>=<pennies>. Gives player the specified number of pennies. The only
thing you can give is pennies.

GO
go <direction>; go home. Goes in the specified direction. 'go home' is a unique
command that returns you to your starting location, except you lose your
possessions. The word go may be omitted.  Move and goto are the same as go.

GRIPE
gripe <message>. Sends <message> to the system maintainer.

HELP
help. This prints a short help message.

INVENTORY
inventory. Lists what you are carrying.

KILL
kill <player> [=<cost>]. Kills the specified player. Killing costs either 10
pennies or <cost>, whichever is greater. The probability of success is directly
proportional to the <cost>. Spending 100 pennies always works.

LOOK
look [<what>].  Prints a description of <what>. Read is the same as look.
<what> can be a room, object, player, or direction (exit).

NEWS
news.  Prints the current news which the system maintainer wants you to know.

NOTE
note <player>=<message>. Creates a object named "Note from <your name>", sets
the success string to <message>, sets the description to "Note to <player>",
and locks the object to <player>.  It's a shorthand for making notes to others.

PAGE
page <player>. This tells a player that you are looking for them. They will get
a message telling them your name and location. This costs 1 penny.

ROB
rob <player>. Attempts to steal one penny from <player>. The only thing you can
rob from players are pennies.  You cannot rob objects, rooms, or exits.

SAY
say <message>. Says message. You can also useen never could sleep
at nite.  I layed there with the covers up around my neck worried that Barnabas
 would come to bite me.  :-)  It was great.


